Market Trends
Digital Retrofit and IoT Integration

The Elevators Modernization Market is being reshaped by a wave of digital retrofit solutions that embed sensors, cloud connectivity, and predictive maintenance algorithms into ageing conveyance systems. Building owners recognise that a connected elevator not only reduces unscheduled downtime but also delivers data that can be leveraged for space utilisation planning and tenant experience management. Consequently, retrofit contracts now frequently include a software layer alongside mechanical upgrades, allowing operators to monitor motor health, door cycle times and passenger loads in real time. This convergence of hardware and data analytics creates a new revenue stream for service providers while aligning with owners’ goals of operational transparency and cost predictability.
Other Trends
Energy Efficiency Upgrades
Energy consumption forms a noticeable slice of a building’s total operating expense, and modern elevators are a prime target for reduction. The market increasingly features regenerative drives that capture braking energy and feed it back into the building’s power grid, as well as LED based cabin lighting and standby mode controllers that curtail power draw when the car is idle. These measures not only lower utility bills but also support green building certifications, which are becoming mandatory in many metropolitan jurisdictions. Retrofitting older machines with such components typically yields a 10 15 % improvement in energy performance, making the investment financially attractive even before any regulatory incentive is considered.
Regulatory Pressure and Safety Standards
Across Europe, North America and parts of Asia, safety codes are tightening around lift inspection intervals, emergency communication systems and fire rating requirements. The market responds by prioritising upgrades that meet the latest EN 81 20/50 and ASME A17.1 standards. Installation of advanced fire rescue operation modes, interoperable emergency phones and redundant braking systems has moved from optional to essential in most new retrofit bids. For owners, compliance translates into avoided penalties and reduced liability, while for manufacturers it drives the development of modular safety kits that can be installed with minimal disruption to building occupants.
Regional Analysis: Elevators Modernization Market
North America
The convergence of ageing infrastructure and heightened safety expectations is reshaping the elevator ecosystem across the United States and Canada. Building owners are increasingly compelled to replace outdated control systems with digital interfaces that improve reliability while meeting stricter compliance benchmarks. This shift is amplified by municipal programmes that subsidise retrofit projects, encouraging property managers to prioritise modernisation over new installations. Consequently, service providers are tailoring value added packages that bundle predictive maintenance analytics with hardware upgrades, positioning themselves as long term partners rather than mere equipment vendors. The strategic emphasis on sustainability-particularly energy efficient drives and regenerative braking-reinforces the market’s move toward greener operations, creating a fertile environment for innovative OEMs to capture premium contracts.
Regulatory Landscape
State and provincial codes have tightened tolerances for vibration and emergency communication, prompting owners to seek upgrades that align with the latest safety standards. Compliance audits are now routine, turning retrofits into a preventive strategy rather than a reactive fix, and driving demand for certified modernisers.
Technology Adoption
IoT enabled controllers are replacing legacy relay systems, offering real time diagnostics that reduce downtime. The region’s high bandwidth availability accelerates the rollout of cloud based monitoring platforms, enabling service firms to shift toward outcome based contracts.
Key Projects
Major urban renewal initiatives in New York, Chicago and Toronto feature extensive elevator overhauls, often bundled with façade upgrades. These projects serve as showcase engagements for manufacturers introducing modular shaft less designs that minimise construction disruption.
Investment Climate
Private equity funds are allocating capital to niche modernisers that demonstrate scalable service platforms. The steady flow of capital reduces financing frictions for large scale retrofit programmes, allowing owners to spread costs over the asset’s remaining lifespan.
Europe
European cities confront a dense stock of historic buildings where original lift installations often predate modern safety directives. Heritage preservation laws restrict structural alterations, nudging owners toward compact retrofit solutions that fit within existing shafts. Simultaneously, the EU’s energy efficiency directives reward upgrades that lower power consumption, prompting a surge in demand for regenerative drives. Market participants are therefore focusing on low profile motor replacements that meet both aesthetic and environmental criteria, positioning themselves to win contracts in capital intensive renovation programmes across London, Berlin and Paris.
Asia Pacific
Rapid urbanisation across China, India and Southeast Asia has produced a paradox of new high rise construction alongside a legacy of mid century towers awaiting modernisation. Developers seeking to differentiate their projects are embedding smart lift ecosystems that integrate with building management systems, a trend amplified by rising consumer expectations for seamless vertical mobility. Moreover, government incentives aimed at reducing carbon footprints are encouraging the adoption of energy saving retrofits, creating a fertile ground for OEMs that can deliver cost effective, high performance solutions at scale.
South America
In Brazil, Argentina and Chile, the elevator fleet is ageing rapidly, yet fiscal constraints limit extensive capital expenditures. Operators are therefore favouring incremental modernisation-such as upgrading control panels and installing destination dispatch algorithms-that extend service life without large upfront outlays. The region’s growing middle class fuels demand for premium residential towers where modernised elevators become a selling point, prompting a gradual shift from cost focused to experience driven refurbishment strategies.
Middle East & Africa
The Gulf Cooperation Council’s ultra luxury developments showcase the latest in elevator technology, yet many older commercial complexes across the region lag behind. Skyscraper owners are motivated by brand perception to undertake high visibility modernisations that incorporate glass cab designs and AI driven traffic management. In Africa, limited electricity reliability drives interest in regenerative systems that feed energy back to the grid, while donor backed infrastructure programmes create modest yet steady demand for safety centric upgrades in public facilities.
